.TH ICONVTEST 1
.SH NAME
iconvtest \- regression test for iconv(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iconvtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iconvtest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/iconv.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(display,\ args,\ inpath,\ outpath) ,
and checks it against golden output for: an identity conversion (no
.BR -c )
round-tripping pixel bytes exactly;
.B -c
with the source's own channel descriptor, also an exact round-trip;
.BR -u 's
documented header format followed by exact raw pixel bytes; a real
conversion to a different channel format, checked by shape (channel
descriptor, depth, rectangle) rather than exact pixel values; an
unrecognised option; and a malformed
.B -c
descriptor.
.PP
Like
.IR splittest (1)
and
.IR jointest (1),
this test drives
.I iconv
entirely with real files, since its whole job is real image files: it
builds a small, fully hand-specified 2x2 image (exact byte values, not
drawn from anything, so the identity checks have a real, controlled
expected value), writes it, calls
.BR run ,
and reads the result back \(em removing every file it created when
done, whether or not anything failed.
.PP
This test does not re-verify Inferno's own pixel-channel conversion
math \(em
.IR iconv (1)
wires together pre-existing, already-shipped
.B Draw
module functionality rather than reimplementing an image format (see
its own BUGS), so the one case here that performs a real conversion to
a different channel format is checked only for the shape it should
produce, not exact pixel values, which would just be re-testing
.B Image.draw
itself.
.PP
Prints one line per failure to standard error, a final
.IR N " passed, " N " failed"
summary to standard output, and exits with a non-nil status if
anything failed.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/iconvtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR iconv (1)
.SH BUGS
Runs
.I iconv
in-process via its
.B run
seam, which takes an already-open
.B Display
and real file paths directly \(em so it does exercise real image
allocation, conversion, and file I/O, but not
.IR iconv 's
own
.BR init ,
its standard-input/standard-output defaulting, or its window-system
.B Context
handling, none of which
.B run
goes through.
